2026-06-30 15:09:56,675 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-dd3d288a-70ed-4432-b0b3-1b869238098b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nB190124_11_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX27.raw\nB190124_12_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX26.raw\nB190124_13_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX25.raw\nB190124_14_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX24.raw\nB190124_15_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX23.raw\nB190124_17_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX22.raw\nB190124_18_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX22.raw\nB190124_19_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX21.raw\nB190124_20_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX21.raw\nB190124_21_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX20.raw\nB190124_22_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX19.raw\nB190124_23_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX18.raw\nB190124_24_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX17.raw\nB190124_25_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condesin_SDA_SCX16.raw\nB191125_02_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC6_rep1.raw\nB191125_03_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC6_rep2.raw\nB191125_04_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC7_rep1.raw\nB191125_05_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC7_rep2.raw\nB191125_06_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC8_rep1.raw\nB191125_07_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC8_rep2.raw\nB191125_08_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC9_rep1.raw\nB191125_09_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC9_rep2.raw\nB191125_10_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC10_rep1.raw\nB191125_11_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC10_rep2.raw\nB191125_14_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC5_rep1.raw\nB191125_15_Lumos_FO_CO_120_Condensin_WTplus_ADP_SDA_pepSEC5_rep2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCryo-EM structures of holo condensin reveal a subunit flip-flop mechanism\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nComplexes containing a pair of SMC family proteins are fundamental for the 3D organization of genomes in all domains of life. The eukaryotic SMC complexes cohesin and condensin are thought to fold, respectively, interphase and mitotic chromosomes into large loop domains, although the underlying molecular mechanisms have remained unknown. We used electron cryo-microscopy to investigate the nucleotide-driven reaction cycle of condensin from the budding yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Our structures of the five-subunit condensin holo complex at different functional stages suggest that ATP binding induces the transition of the SMC coiled coils from a folded-rod conformation into a more open architecture. ATP binding simultaneously triggers the exchange of the two HEAT-repeat subunits bound to the SMC ATPase head domains. We propose that these steps result in the interconversion of DNA binding sites in the catalytic core of condensin that form the basis of the DNA translocation and loop-extrusion activities.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nCryo-EM grid preparation\nFor the collection of condensin apo state datasets, aliquots of purified condensin tetramer or pentamer samples (see Supplementary Note) were thawed and injected onto a Superose 6 Increase 3.2/300 column (GE Healthcare) in nucleotide-free buffer (25 mM Tris-HCl, 125 mM NaCl, 1 mM TCEP, pH 7.5), prior to applying samples onto EM grids. Peak fractions were immediately used for cryo-EM grid preparations, using 3–3.5 μL of sample at a concentration of 0.15–0.25 mg/mL that was applied to freshly glow-discharged Cu/Rh 2/2 holey carbon 200 mesh grids (Quantifoil). The grids were blotted for 1.5–2 s at 4 °C and 100 % humidity, and were flash frozen using an FEI Vitrobot Mark IV (Thermo Fisher) and a liquid-ethane cryostat set to –180 °C. For the collection of +ATP-state datasets, purified S. cerevisiae condensin pentamer complexes (see Supplementary Note) were diluted into 25 mM HEPES-NaOH pH 7.5, 125 mM NaCl, 1 mM MgCl2 and 1 mM DTT to a final concentration of 0.4 μM. After incubation with a mixture of 1 mM ATP and 1 mM AMP-PNP for 10 min, 3 μL were applied onto an UltrAufoil mesh 200 R1.2/1.3 grid (Quantifoil). Plunge freezing was carried out at 10 °C and 100 % humidity on an FEI Vitrobot Mark IV (Thermo Fisher) with a wait time of 2 min, blot force 3 and blot time 3. The blotting paper for the bottom side of the grid was replaced by a piece of Teflon to achieve one-sided blotting.\nCryo-EM data collection\nPlease refer to Tables 1 and 2 for data statistics. For the apo states, images were recorded on a Titan Krios electron microscope (FEI) equipped with a K2 or K3 summit direct electron detector (Gatan), mounted behind a GIF Quantum energy filter. Images were collected automatically using EPU (Thermo Fisher) or SerialEM. A total of 13,628 micrographs of condensin tetramer grids were collected with the K2 camera in counting mode through 8 separate data collection sessions with total doses of 44–55 electrons per Å2 during exposure times of 8–12 s, dose-fractionated into 40–55 movie frames, at defocus ranges of 1.6–3.6 μm. The magnifications used were 105,000× or 130,000×, resulting in physical pixel sizes of 1.16, 1.15, 1.047, 1.055, 1.065 Å per pixel. Three of the eight tetramer datasets with the K2 camera were collected at tilts of 40, 30 and 35°, respectively. ~10,500 micrographs of condensin tetramer grids were collected using the K3 camera in super-resolution mode (at a pixel size of 1.085 Å per pixel) with a total dose of 40 electrons per Å2 during a total exposure time of 2.25 s, dose-fractionated into 40 movie frames, at a defocus range of 1.8–3.3 μm. A total of ~10,000 micrographs of condensin pentamer grids were collected with the K2 camera and a Volta phase plate (VPP) in counting mode (at a pixel size of 1.065 Å per pixel) with a total dose of 45 electrons per Å2 during a total exposure time of 9 s, dose-fractionated into 35–42 movie frames. A defocus range of 0.5–0.9 μm was used for VPP data collection. For the +ATP state, two datasets (4,158 micrographs and 4,894 micrographs, respectively) were acquired on a Titan Krios electron microscope (FEI) running at 300 kV, using a Gatan K2 detector in counting mode and a Gatan GIF energy filter with a slit width of 20 eV. A nominal defocus range of –1 to –2.5 μm was used with a total electron dose of 45 electrons per Å2 divided over 40 frames (0.5 s/frame) and a pixel size of 1.7 Å/pixel (81,000× magnification). Since the data showed strong orientation bias, the stage was tilted by 30° during acquisition.\nImage processing and 3D reconstruction\nFor the apo state, image processing was done in RELION 3.0. Movies were aligned using 5 × 5 patches in MotionCor2 with dose-weighting. CTF parameters were estimated with Gctf. For tilt datasets, local CTF estimation was performed using Gctf after initial processing. All refinements were performed using independent data half-sets (gold-standard refinement) and resolutions were determined based on the Fourier shell correlation (FSC = 0.143) criterion. A tetramer dataset (750 images) was used for initial model calculation: Particles were picked manually from a few micrographs, and the resulting particles were binned and extracted with a 3602-pixel box size (2 Å/pixel) to generate 2D class averages. Good 2D class averages were used as a template for reference-based auto-picking. The resulting ~83 k particles were subjected to multiple rounds of 2D classifications, and a final ~19 k particles were used to calculate the initial 3D model in RELION (Extended Data Fig. 1b).\nFor each dataset, particle picking was performed separately using the same strategy, starting with auto-picking using initial 2D classes; the resulting particles were then downscaled to 4 Å/pixel (1802-pixel box size) for initial processing. Due to condensin’s elongated shape, most particles were not properly centered after auto-picking and rare views tended to be lost during 2D classification. To resolve this issue, particles were first aligned against the 40 Å low-pass filtered initial model by 3D classification into one class, with a large offset search range (20 pixels) and a limited E-step resolution of 10 Å. The rationale was that particle translations become consistent for all viewing angles in 3D alignment, while translations are consistent only within each class average in 2D classification. Aligned particles were then subjected to 2D classification without alignment to remove contaminations and overlapping particles. After selecting good classes, duplicated particles within 100 Å were removed. For tilted datasets, local CTF estimation was performed with the refined particle coordinates using Gctf, followed by re-extraction of particles. The resulting particles from each dataset were then merged for further processing (~ 2.4 M particle images of tetramer and ~ 1.5 M of pentamer) (Extended Data Fig. 1b). The angular assignments resulting from these initial steps were also used for focused 3D classifications for the condensin arm and head, after re-centering particles. The set of pentamer particle images obtained was subjected to further 3D classification into seven classes with no image alignment, and resulted in five similar shaped classes (apo non-engaged form) and a unique class (apo bridged form). Among the five classes of the apo non-engaged form, the best class with clear density for Smc2, Smc4, Brn1C and Ycs4 consisted of 100,388 particles. This class was selected and re-extracted in a 2402-pixel box (3 Å/pixel) and used for a final round of 3D refinement with a soft-edged mask and applying solvent-flattened FCSs. Post-processing with RELION yielded a final overall map of apo condensin at 8.1 Å resolution (Extended Data Fig. 1c). The class of the apo bridged form was re-extracted in a 2402-pixel box (3 Å/pixel) and the resulting 136,570 particles were subjected to another round of 3D classification into 3 classes. The best class consisting of 37,693 particles was selected and refined to 9.1 Å resolution (Extended Data Fig. 1d).\nFor the processing of the apo non-engaged condensin arm segment, which contains the coiled coil and hinge domains, the particles of the tetramer sample from the initial processing were re-centered and re-extracted with a box size of 3002 pixels (1.187 Å/pixel). An initial model for this part of condensin was produced by removing the density of the heads below the ‘joint’ from the overall apo map using UCSF Chimera. After multiple rounds of 3D classifications and 2D classifications with no image alignments, 636,446 particle images were selected and 3D auto-refined. This resulted in a final map at 5.3 Å resolution (Extended Data Fig. 2). For focused refinement of the apo bridged arm segment, the 136,570 particles were re-centered and re-extracted in a box of 4002 pixels (1.187 Å/pixel). After further 3D and 2D classifications, the final 56,848 particles yielded a 7.8 Å resolution map (Extended Data Fig. 2c,d).\nFor focused refinement of the apo condensin head segment, which contains the Smc2–Smc4 ATPase domains, Brn1 and Ycs4, the particle images from the initial processing were re-centered on the heads and re-extracted in a box size of 3202 pixels (1.2 Å/pixel). Again, an initial model for this part was produced using UCSF Chimera. The re-centered particle images of the tetramer heads (~2.4 M) were subjected to several rounds of 3D classification. The best class consisting of 403,128 particles was 3D refined to 4.6 Å resolution, followed by CTF refinement and Bayesian polishing in RELION, resulting in a final resolution of 4.3 Å. The pentamer dataset (~1.5 M particle images) was processed with the same strategy and resulted in a ~5 Å resolution reconstruction with 172,888 particles. In the pentamer reconstruction, density for Ycg1 was not clearly visible, presumably because of its mobility wi\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
